  "description": "<p>curl -XPUT \"http://localhost:8080/rest/sample\" --data-binary @sample.csv -H \"Content-Type: text/csv\"</p>\n\n<p>Once you PUT that CSV file you end up with an RDFSource Container that has no unique properties, and a bunch of odd (not even accessible) resources in \".well-known/genid\".  And the following registered namespaces:</p>\n\n<p>PREFIX ns004: &lt;<a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/sample#Column%201\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/sample#Column%201</a>&gt;<br/>\nPREFIX ns003: &lt;<a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/sample#Column%202\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/sample#Column%202</a>&gt;<br/>\nPREFIX ns002: &lt;<a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/sample#Column%203\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/sample#Column%203</a>&gt;<br/>\nPREFIX ns001: &lt;<a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/sample#Column%204\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/sample#Column%204</a>&gt;</p>\n\n<p>For me, this was <b>not</b> the expected behavior.  Could we just treat \"text/csv\" as non-RDF?</p>",
